WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.609
One model searches a corpus of millions, and answers yes or no questions.

00:00:04.609 --> 00:00:06.506
How can one cosine do both?

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.096
Hi, I'm Fenna!

00:00:11.096 --> 00:00:15.673
Meta's MetaEncoder turns a thirty billion parameter chat model into an embedding model.

00:00:15.673 --> 00:00:18.489
Let's see how, and what it can decide.

00:00:20.092 --> 00:00:20.696
Chapter one.

00:00:20.696 --> 00:00:22.508
Three ways to score a candidate.

00:00:26.817 --> 00:00:29.585
A cross-encoder reads the request with each candidate.

00:00:29.585 --> 00:00:32.699
A constrained decoder lists every option as a letter.

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:35.813
Both are accurate, and neither can cache a thing.

00:00:36.233 --> 00:00:41.497
A bi-encoder embeds the request and each candidate separately and compares them by cosine.

00:00:41.497 --> 00:00:44.882
Candidates are embedded once, so it scales to millions.

00:00:44.882 --> 00:00:46.762
MetaEncoder is one of these.

00:00:48.217 --> 00:00:48.851
Chapter two.

00:00:48.851 --> 00:00:50.118
One tower, not two.

00:00:55.092 --> 00:00:58.055
Pictures go through Muse Glimmer's own vision tower.

00:00:58.055 --> 00:01:02.499
Every twenty-eight by twenty-eight pixels become one token at the decoder's width.

00:01:02.919 --> 00:01:06.575
Those tokens sit in one sequence with the text: the task

00:01:06.575 --> 00:01:07.904
instruction, then the input.

00:01:07.904 --> 00:01:10.563
The same fifty-two layer decoder reads it all.

00:01:10.983 --> 00:01:12.040
Nothing is averaged.

00:01:12.040 --> 00:01:16.270
Under causal attention, only the last token has seen everything, so its

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:18.032
hidden state is the embedding.

00:01:18.452 --> 00:01:22.115
Version one adds a square head and normalises to unit length.

00:01:22.115 --> 00:01:25.778
Queries, documents and answer options all come out the same way.

00:01:27.842 --> 00:01:30.178
Hand the wrapper a video as a list of frames.

00:01:30.948 --> 00:01:33.671
It keeps eight, evenly spaced, first and last included.

00:01:33.671 --> 00:01:36.091
Each one goes in as its own image.

00:01:36.091 --> 00:01:38.209
There is no temporal pooling at all.

00:01:38.629 --> 00:01:42.227
The decoder reads the frames and the question in order.

00:01:42.227 --> 00:01:46.543
Training never went past sixteen thousand tokens, and inputs are never truncated.

00:01:46.964 --> 00:01:51.475
Out come six thousand six hundred and fifty-six numbers: about twenty-six kilobytes

00:01:51.475 --> 00:01:54.483
in float thirty-two, for every vector you store.

00:01:56.342 --> 00:01:59.737
This is what the model sees: eight frames of a test clip.

00:01:59.737 --> 00:02:03.698
Asked where the dog is sitting, it puts almost all its probability on couch.

00:02:05.717 --> 00:02:06.389
Chapter three.

00:02:06.389 --> 00:02:08.068
A decision is a retrieval.

00:02:12.742 --> 00:02:15.648
Here is a choice question from the model card.

00:02:15.648 --> 00:02:19.200
The request and each option are embedded separately, by the same

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:20.491
network, as unit vectors.

00:02:20.911 --> 00:02:24.407
The option pointing most nearly the same way as the request

00:02:24.407 --> 00:02:25.678
has the largest cosine.

00:02:25.678 --> 00:02:26.950
That is the answer.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:31.959
To turn cosines into probabilities, divide by the training temperature, point zero

00:02:31.959 --> 00:02:33.871
two, and take a softmax.

00:02:33.871 --> 00:02:36.930
A small cosine gap becomes a confident answer.

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:41.435
Raise the temperature and the bars flatten, but the winner never moves.

00:02:41.435 --> 00:02:45.179
Calibration changes how sure the model sounds, never what it answers.

00:02:46.817 --> 00:02:47.941
Here is the formula.

00:02:48.817 --> 00:02:50.713
Training uses the same softmax.

00:02:50.713 --> 00:02:53.367
Each request is scored against its candidates.

00:02:53.787 --> 00:02:56.371
Unlike CLIP, it runs in one direction only.

00:02:56.371 --> 00:02:59.601
A candidate like No never has to find its question.

00:03:00.021 --> 00:03:03.684
For search, the negatives are everyone else's documents in the batch.

00:03:03.684 --> 00:03:07.014
For a decision, they are the row's own wrong options.

00:03:07.014 --> 00:03:08.679
The loss is the evaluation.

00:03:10.092 --> 00:03:10.717
Chapter four.

00:03:10.717 --> 00:03:12.593
Putting the options in the question.

00:03:16.717 --> 00:03:19.148
A bi-encoder's request never sees the options.

00:03:19.148 --> 00:03:21.232
So list them in the prompt.

00:03:21.232 --> 00:03:25.399
On MMLU that lifts accuracy from point five six to point seven

00:03:25.399 --> 00:03:28.178
five, and letter plus text adds three points.

00:03:30.092 --> 00:03:30.709
Chapter five.

00:03:30.709 --> 00:03:31.943
What the numbers say.

00:03:36.342 --> 00:03:40.203
This table on the card is all the evidence for version one.

00:03:40.203 --> 00:03:43.099
On decisions and multiple choice, it leads most rows.

00:03:43.519 --> 00:03:45.588
Retrieval is closer than the post says.

00:03:45.588 --> 00:03:49.135
A clear win on text search, a whisker on images, a tie

00:03:49.135 --> 00:03:53.273
on video, and a loss on documents, against a model a third its size.

00:03:54.842 --> 00:03:59.098
And on the hardest decision split, version one went backwards.

00:03:59.098 --> 00:04:02.502
Eighty-two right became seventy-six, while Open-Jev gets eighty-five.

00:04:04.467 --> 00:04:05.127
Chapter six.

00:04:05.127 --> 00:04:06.778
Where one vector runs out.

00:04:11.342 --> 00:04:14.382
The paper tests itself against its own base model.

00:04:14.382 --> 00:04:18.434
Allowed to think before answering, the decoder jumps from about point five

00:04:18.434 --> 00:04:20.461
to point seven two on MMMU.

00:04:20.881 --> 00:04:24.994
Give MetaEncoder that reasoning as context and it climbs from point five

00:04:24.994 --> 00:04:26.708
seven to point seven one.

00:04:26.708 --> 00:04:29.450
One forward pass cannot do the thinking itself.

00:04:31.192 --> 00:04:32.948
Three things to know before you use it.

00:04:33.781 --> 00:04:36.024
The paper describes the earlier checkpoint.

00:04:36.024 --> 00:04:38.640
Version one's evidence is a single picture.

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:44.634
The evaluation repository's own reference file puts video retrieval four points below the paper.

00:04:45.054 --> 00:04:47.769
And version one ships with no licence at all.

00:04:47.769 --> 00:04:49.880
Version zero is Apache two point oh.

00:04:51.142 --> 00:04:54.090
A decision can be a retrieval over a short list.

00:04:54.090 --> 00:04:57.922
Embed the options once, write them into the question, and pick by cosine.

00:05:00.567 --> 00:05:03.531
One chat model, read at its last token.

00:05:03.531 --> 00:05:05.754
Decisions as a softmax over cosines.

00:05:05.754 --> 00:05:07.977
A fast first pass, reasoning elsewhere.

00:05:07.977 --> 00:05:09.088
Thanks for watching!

00:05:09.508 --> 00:05:11.565
Every source is in the full article.

00:05:11.565 --> 00:05:12.153
I'm Fenna.

00:05:12.153 --> 00:05:12.447
Bye!
